       _s_e_v_p_-_>_s_i_g_e_v___v_a_l_u_e_._s_i_v_a_l___p_t_r to _l_i_s_t.

       The ggaaii__ssuussppeenndd() function suspends execution of  the  calling  thread,
       waiting  for  the completion of one or more requests in the array _l_i_s_t.
       The _n argument specifies the size of the array _l_i_s_t.  The  call  blocks
       until one of the following occurs:

       •  One or more of the operations in _l_i_s_t completes.

       •  The call is interrupted by a signal that is caught.

       •  The time interval specified in _t_i_m_e_o_u_t elapses.  This argument spec‐
          ifies  a  timeout  in seconds plus nanoseconds (see nnaannoosslleeeepp(2) for
          details of the _t_i_m_e_s_p_e_c structure).  If _t_i_m_e_o_u_t is  NULL,  then  the
          call blocks indefinitely (until one of the events above occurs).

       No  explicit  indication  of  which request was completed is given; you
       must determine  which  request(s)  have  completed  by  iterating  with
       ggaaii__eerrrroorr() over the list of requests.

       The  ggaaii__eerrrroorr() function returns the status of the request _r_e_q: either
       EEAAII__IINNPPRROOGGRREESSSS if the request was not completed yet, 0 if it  was  han‐
       dled  successfully,  or  an  error code if the request could not be re‐
       solved.

       The ggaaii__ccaanncceell() function cancels the request _r_e_q.  If the request  has
       been canceled successfully, the error status of the request will be set
       to EEAAII__CCAANNCCEELLEEDD and normal asynchronous notification will be performed.
       The  request  cannot be canceled if it is currently being processed; in
       that case, it will be handled as if ggaaii__ccaanncceell() has never been called.
       If _r_e_q is NULL, an attempt is made to cancel all  outstanding  requests
       that the process has made.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The ggeettaaddddrriinnffoo__aa() function returns 0 if all of the requests have been
       enqueued successfully, or one of the following nonzero error codes:

       EEAAII__AAGGAAIINN
              The resources necessary to enqueue the look‐up requests were not
              available.   The  application may check the error status of each
              request to determine which ones failed.

       EEAAII__MMEEMMOORRYY
              Out of memory.

       EEAAII__SSYYSSTTEEMM
              _m_o_d_e is invalid.

       The ggaaii__ssuussppeenndd() function returns 0 if at least one of the listed  re‐
       quests  has been completed.  Otherwise, it returns one of the following
       nonzero error codes:

       EEAAII__AAGGAAIINN
              The given timeout expired before any of the  requests  could  be
              completed.

       EEAAII__AALLLLDDOONNEE
              There were no actual requests given to the function.

       EEAAII__IINNTTRR
              A signal has interrupted the function.  Note that this interrup‐
              tion  might have been caused by signal notification of some com‐
              pleted look‐up request.

       The ggaaii__eerrrroorr() function can return EEAAII__IINNPPRROOGGRREESSSS  for  an  unfinished
       look‐up  request,  0 for a successfully completed look‐up (as described
       above), one of the  error  codes  that  could  be  returned  by  ggeettaadd‐‐
       ddrriinnffoo(3),  or the error code EEAAII__CCAANNCCEELLEEDD if the request has been can‐
       celed explicitly before it could be finished.

       The ggaaii__ccaanncceell() function can return one of these values:

       EEAAII__CCAANNCCEELLEEDD
              The request has been canceled successfully.

       EEAAII__NNOOTTCCAANNCCEELLEEDD
              The request has not been canceled.

       EEAAII__AALLLLDDOONNEE
              The request has already completed.

       The ggaaii__ssttrreerrrroorr(3) function translates these error codes  to  a  human
       readable string, suitable for error reporting.

